Task 2.1: Implement US 3.5, recruiter's My Job Postings page (#37)
Purpose of AI Use: Code generation
AI-Suggested Content:
•	Repository query returning only the logged-in recruiter's job listings, newest first.
•	Service method, a /my_job_postings route restricted to recruiters, and a my_job_postings.html page with job cards, Open/Closed status and an empty state.
•	A "My Job Postings" link on the dashboard for recruiters.
Validation: Ran the app locally and tested with no postings, with several postings, with a second recruiter, and as a job seeker (redirected).
Final decision: Kept the AI code. A teammate later found that the "You have N job postings" text failed with 2 or more postings; the fix was merged and I now test with multiple items.
Reflection: The AI could not run the app, so my own testing was necessary to catch errors.

Task 2.2: Implement US 3.3, editing job postings (#35), and fix the salary period bug
Purpose of AI Use: Code generation and debugging
AI-Suggested Content:
•	Edit routes that reuse the existing creation form in "edit mode", pre-filled with the posting's data.
•	An ownership check so a recruiter can only edit their own postings.
•	Pointed out that the job creation code never saved the salary period, and suggested the one-line fix.
Validation: Tested editing, saving, empty required fields, a second recruiter opening the edit link, and creating a job with a salary period. Checked every diff in GitHub Desktop before committing.
Final decision: After a teammate warned that replacing whole files could overwrite others' work, I only added code to existing files instead of replacing them.
Reflection: Small, additive changes make merges easier and keep the commit history clear.

